      "narrative": "The copper lamps flicker as you approach Mara Venn's table. She's been watching you from beneath the brim of her charcoal coat, a half-empty cup of something dark cradled in her fingers. She doesn't flinch when you speak, just sets the cup down with a soft clink.\n\n\"Ash Gate,\" she repeats, the words carrying a dry weight. She leans back, studying you with those sharp eyes. \"It's not *locked*, if that's what you're thinking. Torches still burn in their sconces. The arch still stands. But nothing passes through. Not since the ferryman stopped.\"\n\nShe pauses, letting that land.\n\n\"Old Bram worked the crossing for... years. Decades, maybe. Knew the route blindfolded. Then one morning\u2014three weeks ago, now\u2014they found him slumped against the arch. Cold. No marks, no wounds. Just... gone.\"\n\nShe gestures with two fingers toward the shuttered window. \"Since then? You can walk up to the Gate, touch the stone, shout into it. Nothing comes back. The mist on the other side doesn't shift. No sound. It's like the path just... stopped existing.\"\n\nHer eyes narrow slightly. \"Toran there\"\u2014she nods toward the ink-stained clerk at the bar\u2014\"he kept the lock mechanism clean for years. Says the tumblers still move fine. The Gate *works*. The question is what's on the other side now.\"\n\nShe picks up her cup again, but doesn't drink. \"That's what I know. Not much, and none of it good.\""
    },
