      "narrative": "The door to the Copper Finch groans shut behind you, cutting off the warm lamplight and leaving you in the grey morning. The street is damp, cobblestones glistening from last night's rain. A few stalls are being set up in the distance\u2014merchants going about their business\u2014but the path to the Ash Gate is quieter, fewer people willing to linger near it.\n\nYou walk through a narrow lane that opens onto a wide, empty square. And there it stands.\n\nThe Ash Gate is a cracked black arch, maybe fifteen feet high, set into nothing\u2014no wall, no building, just the arch alone against a pale sky. Its stone is dark and porous, like cooled volcanic rock, and the iron portcullis within is raised, showing only a thick, motionless mist beyond. The mist doesn't swirl or drift. It just *is*, a grey wall that seems to swallow light.\n\nTwo torches burn in rusted sconces on either side of the arch, their flames unnaturally steady despite the morning breeze.\n\nAt the base of the arch, slumped against the left pillar, lies the unmistakable shape of a body. Old Bram. He's been covered with a rough wool blanket, but one hand\u2014grey and still\u2014rests visible on the cobblestones.\n\nA courier in a travel-stained cloak sits on a crate nearby, idly tossing a coin. They look up as you approach."
    },
