      "narrative": "The courier's coin stops mid-toss. They watch you with flat curiosity as you stand before the arch and call out.\n\n\"Old Bram!\"\n\nThe name echoes off the stone, bounces across the empty square, and fades into nothing. The mist beyond the portcullis doesn't stir. No sound returns. Not even a whisper of movement from the other side.\n\nYou wait. The torches burn steady. The grey hand on the cobblestones doesn't twitch. The silence stretches thin, and you find yourself straining\u2014trying to catch something, anything. A footstep. A breath. The scrape of cloth against stone.\n\nNothing.\n\nThe courier clears their throat behind you. \"He's dead, you know. Been dead three weeks. You're not going to get an answer.\"\n\nTheir voice is flat, matter-of-fact, but there's a thread of something underneath\u2014impatience, maybe. Or unease that you're making noise at a gate that should be silent."
