      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door swings open with a familiar groan. Inside, the tavern is waking slowly\u2014a few patrons nursing morning ales, the clatter of a mop bucket from the back, the smell of old smoke and damp wood. Your table stands in the corner near the copper lamp, scarred and empty, waiting.\n\nYou settle into the chair. The wood is worn smooth by years of elbows. Through the grime-streaked window, you can just see the edge of the Ash Gate's black arch against the pale sky.\n\nA moment passes. Then a figure emerges from the back\u2014Mara Venn, wiping her hands on a rag. She spots you, crosses the room, and sets a tankard of water on the table without being asked.\n\n\"Back already.\" She pulls out the chair across from you and sits, studying your face. \"You've got that look. The one that says you found something you didn't expect.\""
